The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
intel_crtc_vblank_off(old_crtc_state);
intel_disable_pipe(old_crtc_state);
```
Reason:
The functions `intel_crtc_vblank_off()` and `intel_disable_pipe()` were being called without checking if the current output type was MST (Multi-Stream Transport). This resulted in these functions being executed twice for the last MST stream—once from the MST code and again from the DDI code—leading to the transcoder clock being turned off while still being referenced by the DDI code, causing hardware instability and an MCE on ICL.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!intel_crtc_has_type(old_crtc_state, INTEL_OUTPUT_DP_MST)) {
    intel_crtc_vblank_off(old_crtc_state);
    intel_disable_pipe(old_crtc_state);
}
```
Reason:
The added check `if (!intel_crtc_has_type(old_crtc_state, INTEL_OUTPUT_DP_MST))` ensures that the functions `intel_crtc_vblank_off()` and `intel_disable_pipe()` are only called when the output type is not MST. This prevents the double execution of these functions for the last MST stream, thereby maintaining the integrity of the transcoder clock and avoiding hardware instability.

